(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2609100100-lookahead-lands-and-lookbehind-is-blocked-upstream")
 (:adr/title "Lookahead lands, and lookbehind is blocked upstream")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
 (:adr/authority "owner instruction, 2026-09-10")
 (:adr/repos "kotoba-lang/pattern, kotoba-lang/amu, kotoba-lang/cssom")
 (:adr/related "adr-2609093400-dot-was-wrong-and-the-suite-was-told-to-look-away, adr-2609093300-captures-and-the-end-of-the-regex-blocker, kotoba-lang/pattern#4, kotoba-lang/amu#915")
 (:adr/body "Continuing into 2026-09-10. After the `.` fix, cssom had two named gaps left:
non-greedy and lookaround. This takes lookaround, and it splits in two.

## Lookahead landed

`op 9 look` costs no input, so like split, jmp, save and the anchors it lives
in the epsilon closure. Its sub-code is THE SAME PROGRAM run from another
program counter -- there is no second machine and no second format:

  base      look(a = base+1, b = base+L+2, c = negated)
  base+1    ... the sub-pattern, L instructions ...
  base+L+1  match          <- ends the nested run
  base+L+2  whatever follows

The terminating `match` is what stops the nested run: op 6 wins and no thread
steps past it, so the sub-code cannot run on into the outer program.

22/22 against JavaScript, including cssom `^0+(?=\\d)`, the negative form,
stacked lookaheads, and one that can never be satisfied.

## Lookbehind is blocked, and the block is upstream

A lookbehind asks whether the sub-pattern ends here, which needs a backward
scan; the scan needs its own function; that function is the SEVENTH member of
the machine recursion group, and amu runs out of heap there.

Filed as kotoba-lang/amu#915 with two reproducers:

  * a synthetic ring of trivial mutually recursive functions OOMs at EXACTLY
    8 while 5, 7, 9, 10, 11 and 12 compile -- a point defect, not a threshold;
  * `pattern-vm` real group compiles at 6 and OOMs at 7, whether the seventh
    is the real `behind-scan` or a one-line dummy that merely joins the cycle.

Mutual recursion itself is admitted and gets a group CID, so this is not a
language limit. It is also not a reason to reshape the feature: the module
that WOULD ship is kept whole, as
`test/fixtures/pattern_vm_with_lookbehind.kotoba`, and
`test/lookbehind_probe.cljs` compiles it EXPECTING FAILURE. The day amu can
build it, the probe goes red and the block is over. That is the
kotoba-clj-to-kotoba rule about machine-checking a removal condition, applied
to a block this session created rather than one it inherited.

The probe also compiles the SHIPPING module and requires that to succeed, so
\"the toolchain is broken\" and \"the block is still in place\" cannot return the
same answer. It has a third exit code for the first case.

## Measured

  pattern-vm selfcheck     18/18
  pattern-emit selfcheck   19/19
  lookahead vs JavaScript  22/22
  vm parity               320/320
  pattern parity          284/284
  emit parity             168/168  + 6 ahead of the .cljc oracle
  compile parity          157/157
  capture parity         1278/1278
  expressible                      `:refused/lookahead` LEFT the ratchet
  lookbehind probe                 still blocked, as expected

## The break that did not discriminate

\"Ahead of the .cljc oracle\" is now counted per named feature, and the comment
claimed that stops the category becoming a drawer for anything unexplained.
Replacing the lookahead predicate with a catch-all `(fn [re] true)` changes
NOTHING: every pattern that reaches that branch today already matches a named
feature, so the guard is structural rather than demonstrated.

That is recorded in the file as unexercised rather than asserted as working.
A trace never taken cannot be told from one that cannot be taken -- the
workspace rule about stub logs, in a place it does not usually get applied:
to a claim in a comment about a test.

## The third obsolete assertion

`test-refusals` asserted `(?=a)` was refused; it now asserts `(?<=a)` is.
That is the third assertion this week that pinned a CEILING as though it were
a decision -- `(?m)` yesterday, `.` and newline the same day, `(?=` today.
All three were written by someone recording what the code did at the time, in
good faith, in a test whose job is to notice change.

The pattern worth carrying: an assertion that a thing is REFUSED is a claim
about the implementation, not about the language, unless the refusal has a
derivation. Write the reason next to it, so the next reader can tell which
kind it is without measuring.

## Where the goal stands

htmldom is fully expressible and answers identically to JavaScript. cssom has
two gaps left, both now with a named reason:

  * non-greedy (3 patterns) -- a SEMANTICS decision, not an implementation
    task. Under leftmost-longest `*?` and `*` cannot differ, so supporting it
    means either giving the VM priority semantics -- which changes every
    answer the parity suites currently pin, including the POSIX/Perl probe --
    or refusing it permanently and rewriting the three patterns.
  * lookbehind (1 pattern) -- blocked on kotoba-lang/amu#915, with the probe
    holding the removal condition.")))
